1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,904
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,084 --> 00:00:03,930
Here's an uncomfortable idea.

3
00:00:04,110 --> 00:00:06,212
A lot of people who feel comfortable

4
00:00:06,212 --> 00:00:08,431
with money are actually much closer to

5
00:00:08,431 --> 00:00:09,949
trouble than they realize.

6
00:00:10,129 --> 00:00:11,592
They have a nice car,

7
00:00:11,592 --> 00:00:12,707
a decent salary,

8
00:00:12,707 --> 00:00:15,075
a phone that costs more than their

9
00:00:15,075 --> 00:00:15,772
first car.

10
00:00:15,952 --> 00:00:18,684
And underneath, there's almost nothing holding it

11
00:00:18,684 --> 00:00:19,075
all up.

12
00:00:19,255 --> 00:00:21,280
Today, twenty one signs you might be

13
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:22,517
poorer than you think.

14
00:00:22,697 --> 00:00:23,556
And don't worry.

15
00:00:23,736 --> 00:00:25,594
Every sign comes with a fix.

16
00:00:25,943 --> 00:00:28,091
Here's a number that surprises people.

17
00:00:28,271 --> 00:00:31,183
According to the Bureau of Economic Analysis,

18
00:00:31,183 --> 00:00:33,642
the personal saving rate in the United

19
00:00:33,642 --> 00:00:36,100
States has been running at only around

20
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:38,753
four to five percent of disposable income

21
00:00:38,753 --> 00:00:39,788
in recent years.

22
00:00:40,139 --> 00:00:43,066
That means the typical American household spends

23
00:00:43,066 --> 00:00:45,566
roughly ninety five cents of every dollar

24
00:00:45,566 --> 00:00:46,419
it takes home.

25
00:00:46,599 --> 00:00:48,242
With that little margin,

26
00:00:48,242 --> 00:00:49,473
a single job loss,

27
00:00:49,473 --> 00:00:51,731
illness or big repair can knock a

28
00:00:51,731 --> 00:00:53,031
family off balance.

29
00:00:53,211 --> 00:00:55,448
And because so many people live this

30
00:00:55,448 --> 00:00:56,752
way, it feels normal.

31
00:00:56,932 --> 00:00:59,186
When everybody around you is stretched,

32
00:00:59,186 --> 00:01:01,671
being stretched doesn't feel like a warning

33
00:01:01,671 --> 00:01:01,960
sign.

34
00:01:02,140 --> 00:01:04,554
That's exactly why these signs are so

35
00:01:04,554 --> 00:01:05,402
easy to miss.

36
00:01:05,752 --> 00:01:07,986
We'll go through these signs from three

37
00:01:07,986 --> 00:01:08,387
angles.

38
00:01:08,567 --> 00:01:10,030
First, the numbers.

39
00:01:10,210 --> 00:01:12,301
Hard measurements you can check in ten

40
00:01:12,301 --> 00:01:12,741
minutes.

41
00:01:12,921 --> 00:01:14,802
Second, the psychology.

42
00:01:14,982 --> 00:01:17,082
The feelings and beliefs that hide the

43
00:01:17,082 --> 00:01:17,524
problem.

44
00:01:17,704 --> 00:01:19,155
And third, the playbook.

45
00:01:19,335 --> 00:01:21,486
The weak spots in your safety net,

46
00:01:21,486 --> 00:01:23,004
and how to fix each one.

47
00:01:23,184 --> 00:01:25,392
If you recognize yourself in a few

48
00:01:25,392 --> 00:01:27,341
of these, that's not bad news.

49
00:01:27,521 --> 00:01:28,519
It's information.

50
00:01:28,699 --> 00:01:31,578
And information is where every turnaround starts.

51
00:01:31,929 --> 00:01:33,554
Let's start with the numbers.

52
00:01:33,734 --> 00:01:34,332
Grab a pen,

53
00:01:34,332 --> 00:01:36,180
because you'll want to check a few

54
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:37,159
of these as we go.

55
00:01:37,509 --> 00:01:39,656
Our numbers come from named sources.

56
00:01:39,836 --> 00:01:42,329
Saving data comes from the Bureau of

57
00:01:42,329 --> 00:01:43,575
Economic Analysis.

58
00:01:43,755 --> 00:01:46,818
Household wealth comes from the Federal Reserve's

59
00:01:46,818 --> 00:01:48,569
Survey of Consumer Finances,

60
00:01:48,569 --> 00:01:51,570
and emergency savings from the Federal Reserve's

61
00:01:51,570 --> 00:01:54,633
Survey of Household Economics and Decisionmaking.

62
00:01:54,813 --> 00:01:57,531
Housing cost burden comes from the Department

63
00:01:57,531 --> 00:02:00,309
of Housing and Urban Development and Harvard's

64
00:02:00,309 --> 00:02:02,302
Joint Center for Housing Studies.

65
00:02:02,482 --> 00:02:04,978
Retirement guidelines come from Fidelity.

66
00:02:05,158 --> 00:02:06,562
This is education,

67
00:02:06,562 --> 00:02:08,200
not financial advice.

68
00:02:08,550 --> 00:02:09,188
Angle one.

69
00:02:09,368 --> 00:02:10,146
The numbers.

70
00:02:10,326 --> 00:02:12,590
Seven measurements that tell the truth,

71
00:02:12,590 --> 00:02:14,564
no matter how things look from the

72
00:02:14,564 --> 00:02:15,028
outside.

73
00:02:15,378 --> 00:02:16,480
Sign number one.

74
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:18,368
You don't know your net worth,

75
00:02:18,368 --> 00:02:20,132
or it's lower than you'd guess.

76
00:02:20,481 --> 00:02:21,677
Net worth is simple.

77
00:02:21,857 --> 00:02:23,086
Everything you own,

78
00:02:23,086 --> 00:02:24,702
minus everything you owe.

79
00:02:24,882 --> 00:02:27,869
Savings, investments, retirement accounts,

80
00:02:27,869 --> 00:02:30,074
the value of your home and car,

81
00:02:30,074 --> 00:02:31,496
minus your mortgage,

82
00:02:31,496 --> 00:02:34,483
car loans, student loans and credit cards.

83
00:02:34,663 --> 00:02:37,275
Many people have never done this calculation.

84
00:02:37,455 --> 00:02:39,113
And many who do are shocked,

85
00:02:39,113 --> 00:02:41,007
because the car they think of as

86
00:02:41,007 --> 00:02:42,842
an asset is worth less than the

87
00:02:42,842 --> 00:02:43,493
loan on it.

88
00:02:43,842 --> 00:02:46,760
The Federal Reserve's Survey of Consumer Finances

89
00:02:46,760 --> 00:02:48,844
found that the median net worth for

90
00:02:48,844 --> 00:02:51,464
families headed by someone under thirty five

91
00:02:51,464 --> 00:02:53,905
was about thirty nine thousand dollars in

92
00:02:53,905 --> 00:02:54,976
twenty twenty two.

93
00:02:55,156 --> 00:02:57,319
Lots of young adults are below zero

94
00:02:57,319 --> 00:02:58,864
because of student loans,

95
00:02:58,864 --> 00:03:00,842
and that's not a disaster on its

96
00:03:00,842 --> 00:03:01,089
own.

97
00:03:01,269 --> 00:03:03,372
The danger is not knowing the number,

98
00:03:03,372 --> 00:03:05,873
because you can't tell whether you're moving

99
00:03:05,873 --> 00:03:07,237
forward or sliding back.

100
00:03:07,586 --> 00:03:08,631
The fix.

101
00:03:08,811 --> 00:03:11,215
Tonight, make a simple list with two

102
00:03:11,215 --> 00:03:11,749
columns.

103
00:03:11,929 --> 00:03:12,794
What you own,

104
00:03:12,794 --> 00:03:13,926
and what you owe.

105
00:03:14,106 --> 00:03:15,364
Use realistic values,

106
00:03:15,364 --> 00:03:17,640
like what your car would actually sell

107
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:18,239
for today.

108
00:03:18,419 --> 00:03:19,243
Subtract.

109
00:03:19,423 --> 00:03:21,176
Write the date next to the answer.

110
00:03:21,356 --> 00:03:23,260
Do it again in three months.

111
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:25,721
The direction of that number matters much

112
00:03:25,721 --> 00:03:27,167
more than where it starts.

113
00:03:27,517 --> 00:03:28,840
Sign number two.

114
00:03:29,020 --> 00:03:31,363
A four hundred dollar surprise would mean

115
00:03:31,363 --> 00:03:31,934
borrowing.

116
00:03:32,284 --> 00:03:35,074
The Federal Reserve asks Americans every year

117
00:03:35,074 --> 00:03:37,306
how they would pay for an unexpected

118
00:03:37,306 --> 00:03:39,041
four hundred dollar expense.

119
00:03:39,221 --> 00:03:41,291
In its report for twenty twenty four,

120
00:03:41,291 --> 00:03:43,584
about sixty three percent said they would

121
00:03:43,584 --> 00:03:45,653
cover it with cash or its equivalent.

122
00:03:45,833 --> 00:03:47,476
The rest would need to use a

123
00:03:47,476 --> 00:03:49,764
credit card they couldn't pay off right

124
00:03:49,764 --> 00:03:51,289
away, borrow from someone,

125
00:03:51,289 --> 00:03:53,577
sell something, or simply couldn't pay.

126
00:03:53,927 --> 00:03:56,725
Four hundred dollars is a dentist visit,

127
00:03:56,725 --> 00:03:57,634
a car repair,

128
00:03:57,634 --> 00:03:59,663
a vet bill or a broken phone.

129
00:03:59,843 --> 00:04:01,224
These things are not rare.

130
00:04:01,404 --> 00:04:03,509
They're guaranteed to happen eventually,

131
00:04:03,509 --> 00:04:04,771
we just don't know when.

132
00:04:04,951 --> 00:04:06,767
If one of them sends you into

133
00:04:06,767 --> 00:04:08,832
debt, your budget has no room for

134
00:04:08,832 --> 00:04:09,584
normal life.

135
00:04:09,764 --> 00:04:11,533
And the interest on that debt makes

136
00:04:11,533 --> 00:04:13,049
the next surprise even harder.

137
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:14,352
The fix.

138
00:04:14,532 --> 00:04:16,935
Build a starter emergency fund of one

139
00:04:16,935 --> 00:04:19,988
thousand dollars in a separate savings account.

140
00:04:20,168 --> 00:04:21,797
Set an automatic transfer,

141
00:04:21,797 --> 00:04:23,489
even twenty dollars a week.

142
00:04:23,669 --> 00:04:25,515
Sell one thing you don't use.

143
00:04:25,695 --> 00:04:28,028
Put any refund or bonus there first.

144
00:04:28,208 --> 00:04:30,689
Once you reach one thousand dollars,

145
00:04:30,689 --> 00:04:32,756
aim for one month of expenses,

146
00:04:32,756 --> 00:04:33,514
then three.

147
00:04:33,863 --> 00:04:35,094
Sign number three.

148
00:04:35,274 --> 00:04:37,322
You save less than five percent of

149
00:04:37,322 --> 00:04:38,165
what you earn.

150
00:04:38,515 --> 00:04:41,450
Remember, the national personal saving rate has

151
00:04:41,450 --> 00:04:43,635
been around four to five percent in

152
00:04:43,635 --> 00:04:44,447
recent years.

153
00:04:44,627 --> 00:04:48,091
Many financial planners suggest aiming for fifteen

154
00:04:48,091 --> 00:04:50,861
percent or more toward retirement alone,

155
00:04:50,861 --> 00:04:52,870
including any employer match.

156
00:04:53,050 --> 00:04:55,387
If you're saving less than five percent

157
00:04:55,387 --> 00:04:57,485
in total, you may be falling behind

158
00:04:57,485 --> 00:04:58,623
without feeling it.

159
00:04:58,973 --> 00:05:01,109
Here's why this matters so much.

160
00:05:01,289 --> 00:05:03,508
Your savings rate decides how fast you

161
00:05:03,508 --> 00:05:05,611
build a cushion and how much freedom

162
00:05:05,611 --> 00:05:06,954
you'll eventually have.

163
00:05:07,134 --> 00:05:09,789
Someone saving twenty percent of their pay

164
00:05:09,789 --> 00:05:12,633
builds options far faster than someone saving

165
00:05:12,633 --> 00:05:14,782
two percent, even if they earn the

166
00:05:14,782 --> 00:05:15,540
same salary.

167
00:05:15,720 --> 00:05:17,636
The savings rate is the engine.

168
00:05:17,816 --> 00:05:19,499
Income is just the fuel.

169
00:05:19,850 --> 00:05:20,871
The fix.

170
00:05:21,051 --> 00:05:22,642
Calculate your savings rate.

171
00:05:22,822 --> 00:05:25,693
Add up everything you saved and invested

172
00:05:25,693 --> 00:05:29,067
last month, including retirement contributions,

173
00:05:29,067 --> 00:05:31,436
and divide by your take home pay.

174
00:05:31,616 --> 00:05:34,136
Then raise it by one percentage point.

175
00:05:34,316 --> 00:05:36,185
Next month, another point.

176
00:05:36,365 --> 00:05:39,311
Small increases are much easier to stick

177
00:05:39,311 --> 00:05:40,858
to than one big jump.

178
00:05:41,208 --> 00:05:42,369
Sign number four.

179
00:05:42,549 --> 00:05:45,165
Your housing costs more than thirty percent

180
00:05:45,165 --> 00:05:46,078
of your income.

181
00:05:46,428 --> 00:05:49,013
The Department of Housing and Urban Development

182
00:05:49,013 --> 00:05:51,654
considers households that spend more than thirty

183
00:05:51,654 --> 00:05:53,689
percent of their income on housing to

184
00:05:53,689 --> 00:05:54,624
be cost burdened.

185
00:05:54,804 --> 00:05:57,571
Harvard's Joint Center for Housing Studies has

186
00:05:57,571 --> 00:06:00,158
reported that about half of American renter

187
00:06:00,158 --> 00:06:02,925
households were cost burdened in recent years,

188
00:06:02,925 --> 00:06:03,767
a record high.

189
00:06:03,947 --> 00:06:06,114
Homeowners can be stretched too,

190
00:06:06,114 --> 00:06:09,636
especially with rising insurance and property taxes.

191
00:06:09,986 --> 00:06:11,678
When housing takes too much,

192
00:06:11,678 --> 00:06:13,492
everything else gets squeezed.

193
00:06:13,672 --> 00:06:15,568
Saving becomes impossible,

194
00:06:15,568 --> 00:06:17,756
small surprises become crises,

195
00:06:17,756 --> 00:06:20,527
and people start using credit cards to

196
00:06:20,527 --> 00:06:21,694
cover groceries.

197
00:06:21,874 --> 00:06:24,316
The term house poor describes people who

198
00:06:24,316 --> 00:06:26,574
own a beautiful home but can't afford

199
00:06:26,574 --> 00:06:27,795
to do anything else.

200
00:06:28,146 --> 00:06:29,109
The fix.

201
00:06:29,289 --> 00:06:31,583
Add up your full housing cost,

202
00:06:31,583 --> 00:06:33,647
including rent or mortgage,

203
00:06:33,647 --> 00:06:35,635
insurance, property taxes,

204
00:06:35,635 --> 00:06:37,393
HOA fees and utilities.

205
00:06:37,573 --> 00:06:39,756
Divide by your gross monthly income.

206
00:06:39,936 --> 00:06:41,823
If it's well above thirty percent,

207
00:06:41,823 --> 00:06:43,767
look at options over the next year.

208
00:06:43,947 --> 00:06:46,345
A roommate, a cheaper place when the

209
00:06:46,345 --> 00:06:48,942
lease ends, refinancing if rates allow,

210
00:06:48,942 --> 00:06:51,540
or appealing a property tax assessment.

211
00:06:51,890 --> 00:06:53,236
Sign number five.

212
00:06:53,416 --> 00:06:56,097
Your car loan stretches six years or

213
00:06:56,097 --> 00:06:56,470
more.

214
00:06:56,820 --> 00:06:58,323
Car prices have climbed,

215
00:06:58,323 --> 00:07:00,829
and to keep monthly payments manageable,

216
00:07:00,829 --> 00:07:03,774
lenders have stretched loans longer and longer.

217
00:07:03,954 --> 00:07:06,593
Edmunds has reported that a large share

218
00:07:06,593 --> 00:07:08,758
of new car loans now run seventy

219
00:07:08,758 --> 00:07:10,043
two months or more,

220
00:07:10,043 --> 00:07:12,209
and some run eighty four months.

221
00:07:12,389 --> 00:07:14,864
That's seven years of payments on something

222
00:07:14,864 --> 00:07:16,475
that loses value every year.

223
00:07:16,826 --> 00:07:18,046
The longer the loan,

224
00:07:18,046 --> 00:07:19,633
the more interest you pay,

225
00:07:19,633 --> 00:07:21,769
and the more likely you'll owe more

226
00:07:21,769 --> 00:07:23,722
than the car is worth for years.

227
00:07:23,902 --> 00:07:26,335
That's called being underwater,

228
00:07:26,335 --> 00:07:27,513
or upside down.

229
00:07:27,693 --> 00:07:29,420
If you need to sell or trade

230
00:07:29,420 --> 00:07:31,640
the car, the negative equity can get

231
00:07:31,640 --> 00:07:33,244
rolled into the next loan,

232
00:07:33,244 --> 00:07:34,786
and the hole gets deeper.

233
00:07:35,137 --> 00:07:36,054
The fix.

234
00:07:36,234 --> 00:07:37,978
Look up what your car would sell

235
00:07:37,978 --> 00:07:39,832
for today and compare it with your

236
00:07:39,832 --> 00:07:40,541
loan balance.

237
00:07:40,721 --> 00:07:42,040
If you're underwater,

238
00:07:42,040 --> 00:07:44,050
the best move is usually to keep

239
00:07:44,050 --> 00:07:46,060
the car, pay a little extra each

240
00:07:46,060 --> 00:07:48,259
month, and drive it for years after

241
00:07:48,259 --> 00:07:49,138
it's paid off.

242
00:07:49,318 --> 00:07:51,581
Next time, aim for a loan of

243
00:07:51,581 --> 00:07:53,117
four years or less,

244
00:07:53,117 --> 00:07:55,622
or buy a cheaper car with cash.

245
00:07:55,973 --> 00:07:57,377
Sign number six.

246
00:07:57,557 --> 00:07:59,671
You pay only the minimum on your

247
00:07:59,671 --> 00:08:00,530
credit cards.

248
00:08:00,880 --> 00:08:03,220
Credit card statements are required to show

249
00:08:03,220 --> 00:08:04,799
how long it would take to pay

250
00:08:04,799 --> 00:08:06,595
off your balance if you only make

251
00:08:06,595 --> 00:08:07,520
minimum payments.

252
00:08:07,700 --> 00:08:08,890
For many balances,

253
00:08:08,890 --> 00:08:10,541
the answer is many years,

254
00:08:10,541 --> 00:08:12,589
and the total interest can be a

255
00:08:12,589 --> 00:08:15,166
big share of what you originally spent.

256
00:08:15,346 --> 00:08:18,175
With average card rates above twenty percent,

257
00:08:18,175 --> 00:08:20,250
according to the Federal Reserve,

258
00:08:20,250 --> 00:08:22,765
the minimum payment keeps you paying for

259
00:08:22,765 --> 00:08:23,519
a long time.

260
00:08:23,869 --> 00:08:25,921
Paying the minimum feels responsible,

261
00:08:25,921 --> 00:08:27,363
because you're never late.

262
00:08:27,543 --> 00:08:29,204
But it's often a sign that the

263
00:08:29,204 --> 00:08:31,363
card is quietly funding your lifestyle.

264
00:08:31,543 --> 00:08:33,644
If your balance stays the same or

265
00:08:33,644 --> 00:08:35,619
keeps rising month after month,

266
00:08:35,619 --> 00:08:38,230
your spending is larger than your income,

267
00:08:38,230 --> 00:08:40,332
and the card is covering the gap.

268
00:08:40,682 --> 00:08:41,564
The fix.

269
00:08:41,744 --> 00:08:44,358
Stop adding new charges to cards with

270
00:08:44,358 --> 00:08:45,064
a balance.

271
00:08:45,244 --> 00:08:47,266
Then list them by interest rate and

272
00:08:47,266 --> 00:08:49,518
attack the highest one with every extra

273
00:08:49,518 --> 00:08:51,713
dollar while paying the minimum on the

274
00:08:51,713 --> 00:08:52,001
rest.

275
00:08:52,181 --> 00:08:54,024
If your rate is very high,

276
00:08:54,024 --> 00:08:56,291
ask the issuer for a lower rate,

277
00:08:56,291 --> 00:08:59,267
or look into a nonprofit credit counseling

278
00:08:59,267 --> 00:08:59,763
agency.

279
00:09:00,113 --> 00:09:01,401
Sign number seven.

280
00:09:01,581 --> 00:09:04,641
Your retirement savings are far behind common

281
00:09:04,641 --> 00:09:05,389
milestones.

282
00:09:05,740 --> 00:09:08,387
Fidelity offers a widely quoted guideline.

283
00:09:08,567 --> 00:09:10,986
Aim to have about one times your

284
00:09:10,986 --> 00:09:13,859
salary saved for retirement by thirty,

285
00:09:13,859 --> 00:09:15,447
three times by forty,

286
00:09:15,447 --> 00:09:16,884
six times by fifty,

287
00:09:16,884 --> 00:09:18,472
eight times by sixty,

288
00:09:18,472 --> 00:09:20,664
and ten times by sixty seven.

289
00:09:20,844 --> 00:09:22,184
These are rough targets,

290
00:09:22,184 --> 00:09:24,195
not rules, and they assume you start

291
00:09:24,195 --> 00:09:25,535
saving in your twenties.

292
00:09:25,715 --> 00:09:27,758
But they're a useful reality check.

293
00:09:28,108 --> 00:09:30,924
Social Security was designed to replace only

294
00:09:30,924 --> 00:09:33,165
part of your pre retirement income,

295
00:09:33,165 --> 00:09:36,237
and the Social Security Administration says that

296
00:09:36,237 --> 00:09:38,926
for the average worker it replaces roughly

297
00:09:38,926 --> 00:09:39,822
forty percent.

298
00:09:40,002 --> 00:09:41,946
If you're counting on it to cover

299
00:09:41,946 --> 00:09:44,007
everything, the gap can be painful.

300
00:09:44,187 --> 00:09:46,581
Being behind isn't a crisis at thirty

301
00:09:46,581 --> 00:09:46,904
five.

302
00:09:47,084 --> 00:09:48,983
Being behind and not knowing it can

303
00:09:48,983 --> 00:09:50,068
become one at sixty.

304
00:09:50,418 --> 00:09:51,497
The fix.

305
00:09:51,677 --> 00:09:54,716
Find your total retirement savings and divide

306
00:09:54,716 --> 00:09:55,729
by your salary.

307
00:09:55,909 --> 00:09:58,031
Compare it with the milestone for your

308
00:09:58,031 --> 00:09:58,254
age.

309
00:09:58,434 --> 00:09:59,413
If you're behind,

310
00:09:59,413 --> 00:10:01,427
make sure you get the full employer

311
00:10:01,427 --> 00:10:03,959
match first, then raise your contribution by

312
00:10:03,959 --> 00:10:05,052
one percent a year.

313
00:10:05,232 --> 00:10:08,469
After fifty, catch up contributions can help,

314
00:10:08,469 --> 00:10:10,770
so check the current IRS limits.

315
00:10:11,120 --> 00:10:13,224
Let's turn those seven numbers into a

316
00:10:13,224 --> 00:10:15,102
quick checkup you can do tonight.

317
00:10:15,282 --> 00:10:17,314
Grab your phone and a piece of

318
00:10:17,314 --> 00:10:17,720
paper.

319
00:10:17,900 --> 00:10:19,764
First, write your net worth,

320
00:10:19,764 --> 00:10:20,895
even a rough one.

321
00:10:21,075 --> 00:10:23,127
Second, write how much cash you could

322
00:10:23,127 --> 00:10:24,790
reach today without borrowing.

323
00:10:24,970 --> 00:10:27,857
Third, write what percentage of last month's

324
00:10:27,857 --> 00:10:30,218
take home pay you saved or invested.

325
00:10:30,398 --> 00:10:33,115
That's three numbers in about five minutes.

326
00:10:33,465 --> 00:10:36,271
Fourth, divide your total housing cost by

327
00:10:36,271 --> 00:10:38,050
your gross monthly income.

328
00:10:38,230 --> 00:10:40,685
Fifth, write the number of months left

329
00:10:40,685 --> 00:10:41,783
on your car loan.

330
00:10:41,963 --> 00:10:44,738
Sixth, write whether you paid your credit

331
00:10:44,738 --> 00:10:46,430
cards in full last month,

332
00:10:46,430 --> 00:10:47,106
yes or no.

333
00:10:47,286 --> 00:10:50,195
And seventh, divide your retirement savings by

334
00:10:50,195 --> 00:10:51,396
your yearly salary.

335
00:10:51,576 --> 00:10:53,359
Seven numbers, ten minutes,

336
00:10:53,359 --> 00:10:55,735
and you'll know more about your real

337
00:10:55,735 --> 00:10:58,507
financial health than most people ever do.

338
00:10:58,858 --> 00:11:00,355
Keep that piece of paper.

339
00:11:00,535 --> 00:11:01,557
Put a date on it.

340
00:11:01,737 --> 00:11:02,652
In six months,

341
00:11:02,652 --> 00:11:04,024
do the checkup again.

342
00:11:04,204 --> 00:11:05,690
You're not looking for perfection.

343
00:11:05,870 --> 00:11:07,124
You're looking for direction.

344
00:11:07,304 --> 00:11:09,336
If four of those seven numbers moved

345
00:11:09,336 --> 00:11:10,126
the right way,

346
00:11:10,126 --> 00:11:12,610
you're winning, no matter where you started.

347
00:11:12,790 --> 00:11:14,853
And that sense of progress is one

348
00:11:14,853 --> 00:11:16,853
of the best motivators there is.

349
00:11:17,204 --> 00:11:19,157
Here's why the earlier you fix these

350
00:11:19,157 --> 00:11:20,187
numbers the better.

351
00:11:20,367 --> 00:11:22,510
Two hundred fifty dollars a month,

352
00:11:22,510 --> 00:11:24,778
invested for thirty years at a seven

353
00:11:24,778 --> 00:11:26,669
percent average yearly return,

354
00:11:26,669 --> 00:11:29,441
grows to roughly three hundred five thousand

355
00:11:29,441 --> 00:11:29,946
dollars.

356
00:11:30,126 --> 00:11:31,902
You'd put in ninety thousand.

357
00:11:32,252 --> 00:11:33,192
That's angle one.

358
00:11:33,372 --> 00:11:34,533
Seven numbers.

359
00:11:34,713 --> 00:11:37,151
If several of them made you uncomfortable,

360
00:11:37,151 --> 00:11:37,441
good.

361
00:11:37,621 --> 00:11:39,177
That discomfort is useful.

362
00:11:39,357 --> 00:11:40,523
Now angle two,

363
00:11:40,523 --> 00:11:41,772
the psychology.

364
00:11:41,952 --> 00:11:44,532
Because the numbers are often hidden behind

365
00:11:44,532 --> 00:11:46,933
feelings that tell us everything's fine.

366
00:11:47,283 --> 00:11:49,538
Here's the strange thing about being poorer

367
00:11:49,538 --> 00:11:50,324
than you think.

368
00:11:50,504 --> 00:11:52,641
It often doesn't feel like being poor.

369
00:11:52,821 --> 00:11:54,469
Your card still works.

370
00:11:54,649 --> 00:11:56,367
Your paycheck still arrives.

371
00:11:56,547 --> 00:11:58,417
You can still go out to dinner.

372
00:11:58,597 --> 00:12:00,866
The feeling of being okay comes from

373
00:12:00,866 --> 00:12:02,567
cash flow, not from wealth.

374
00:12:02,747 --> 00:12:04,656
And cash flow can disappear in a

375
00:12:04,656 --> 00:12:06,625
single phone call from your boss.

376
00:12:06,975 --> 00:12:08,194
Sign number eight.

377
00:12:08,374 --> 00:12:10,028
You feel rich for a few days

378
00:12:10,028 --> 00:12:12,391
after payday, and broke the week before.

379
00:12:12,741 --> 00:12:14,740
This boom and bust cycle is one

380
00:12:14,740 --> 00:12:17,319
of the clearest signs of living paycheck

381
00:12:17,319 --> 00:12:18,093
to paycheck.

382
00:12:18,273 --> 00:12:20,681
Right after payday the account looks full,

383
00:12:20,681 --> 00:12:22,058
and spending feels easy.

384
00:12:22,238 --> 00:12:23,631
By the end of the month,

385
00:12:23,631 --> 00:12:26,707
you're checking your balance before buying groceries.

386
00:12:27,058 --> 00:12:28,822
The problem isn't how much you earn.

387
00:12:29,002 --> 00:12:31,556
It's that your spending follows your balance

388
00:12:31,556 --> 00:12:32,601
instead of a plan.

389
00:12:32,781 --> 00:12:34,434
When money is there,

390
00:12:34,434 --> 00:12:35,591
it gets spent.

391
00:12:35,771 --> 00:12:38,747
People on very different incomes report this

392
00:12:38,747 --> 00:12:41,249
same feeling, which shows that it's a

393
00:12:41,249 --> 00:12:43,143
pattern, not a salary level.

394
00:12:43,494 --> 00:12:44,411
The fix.

395
00:12:44,591 --> 00:12:46,773
Make your account look emptier on payday.

396
00:12:46,953 --> 00:12:48,599
Move savings out first,

397
00:12:48,599 --> 00:12:49,600
automatically.

398
00:12:49,780 --> 00:12:51,807
Put money for fixed bills into a

399
00:12:51,807 --> 00:12:53,263
separate bills account.

400
00:12:53,443 --> 00:12:55,497
What's left in your checking account is

401
00:12:55,497 --> 00:12:56,971
what you can actually spend,

402
00:12:56,971 --> 00:12:58,656
and it can last the whole month.

403
00:12:59,007 --> 00:13:00,388
Sign number nine.

404
00:13:00,568 --> 00:13:02,354
You measure how you're doing by what

405
00:13:02,354 --> 00:13:02,751
you own.

406
00:13:03,101 --> 00:13:04,014
A big TV,

407
00:13:04,014 --> 00:13:05,029
a new SUV,

408
00:13:05,029 --> 00:13:08,174
nice clothes, the latest phone.

409
00:13:08,354 --> 00:13:10,606
These things can make you feel successful.

410
00:13:10,786 --> 00:13:12,696
But most of them lose value fast,

411
00:13:12,696 --> 00:13:14,606
and many were bought with credit.

412
00:13:14,786 --> 00:13:17,977
Thomas Stanley's research on millionaires found that

413
00:13:17,977 --> 00:13:20,125
many people who look rich have very

414
00:13:20,125 --> 00:13:21,291
little real wealth,

415
00:13:21,291 --> 00:13:24,666
while many quiet millionaires look completely ordinary.

416
00:13:25,017 --> 00:13:28,008
Stanley called the first group income statement

417
00:13:28,008 --> 00:13:28,581
affluent.

418
00:13:28,761 --> 00:13:30,317
They earn and spend a lot.

419
00:13:30,497 --> 00:13:32,838
He called the second group balance sheet

420
00:13:32,838 --> 00:13:33,364
affluent.

421
00:13:33,544 --> 00:13:35,088
They own assets that grow.

422
00:13:35,268 --> 00:13:37,137
If your sense of success comes from

423
00:13:37,137 --> 00:13:38,525
what other people can see,

424
00:13:38,525 --> 00:13:40,447
you might be building the wrong kind

425
00:13:40,447 --> 00:13:40,980
of wealth.

426
00:13:41,331 --> 00:13:42,364
The fix.

427
00:13:42,544 --> 00:13:43,798
Change your scoreboard.

428
00:13:43,978 --> 00:13:45,720
Instead of counting things,

429
00:13:45,720 --> 00:13:47,786
track three numbers every month.

430
00:13:47,966 --> 00:13:49,607
Net worth, savings rate,

431
00:13:49,607 --> 00:13:52,273
and months of expenses you could cover.

432
00:13:52,453 --> 00:13:54,102
Celebrate when those go up,

433
00:13:54,102 --> 00:13:55,994
not when you buy something new.

434
00:13:56,344 --> 00:13:57,667
Sign number ten.

435
00:13:57,847 --> 00:14:00,179
You're spending money you expect to earn

436
00:14:00,179 --> 00:14:00,529
later.

437
00:14:00,879 --> 00:14:02,779
A raise you're hoping for,

438
00:14:02,779 --> 00:14:04,972
a bonus that isn't guaranteed,

439
00:14:04,972 --> 00:14:05,923
a tax refund,

440
00:14:05,923 --> 00:14:07,531
a promotion next year.

441
00:14:07,711 --> 00:14:10,289
Many people make today's purchases based on

442
00:14:10,289 --> 00:14:11,368
tomorrow's income.

443
00:14:11,548 --> 00:14:13,103
When that money arrives,

444
00:14:13,103 --> 00:14:14,335
it's already spent.

445
00:14:14,515 --> 00:14:16,169
And if it doesn't arrive,

446
00:14:16,169 --> 00:14:17,162
the debt stays.

447
00:14:17,512 --> 00:14:19,259
This is how buy now pay later

448
00:14:19,259 --> 00:14:21,006
and credit cards hook people.

449
00:14:21,186 --> 00:14:22,731
They let you live in the future,

450
00:14:22,731 --> 00:14:23,021
today.

451
00:14:23,201 --> 00:14:24,123
The trouble is,

452
00:14:24,123 --> 00:14:26,092
the future has its own expenses.

453
00:14:26,272 --> 00:14:29,089
Jobs change, companies cut bonuses,

454
00:14:29,089 --> 00:14:31,183
and life throws surprises.

455
00:14:31,363 --> 00:14:33,962
Spending money you don't have yet turns

456
00:14:33,962 --> 00:14:35,229
optimism into risk.

457
00:14:35,579 --> 00:14:36,693
The fix.

458
00:14:36,873 --> 00:14:39,532
Only spend money that has already arrived.

459
00:14:39,712 --> 00:14:40,955
Treat any raise,

460
00:14:40,955 --> 00:14:43,597
bonus or refund as zero until it's

461
00:14:43,597 --> 00:14:45,540
actually in your account.

462
00:14:45,720 --> 00:14:47,244
And when it does arrive,

463
00:14:47,244 --> 00:14:49,593
decide its job before spending any of

464
00:14:49,593 --> 00:14:49,784
it.

465
00:14:50,134 --> 00:14:51,504
Sign number eleven.

466
00:14:51,684 --> 00:14:54,307
You tell yourself everyone lives like this.

467
00:14:54,658 --> 00:14:57,223
It's true that many Americans are stretched.

468
00:14:57,403 --> 00:14:59,307
But normal isn't the same as safe.

469
00:14:59,487 --> 00:15:01,547
If most of your friends have car

470
00:15:01,547 --> 00:15:04,507
payments, credit card balances and no savings,

471
00:15:04,507 --> 00:15:06,244
that becomes your baseline.

472
00:15:06,424 --> 00:15:08,452
You compare yourself with them and feel

473
00:15:08,452 --> 00:15:08,712
fine.

474
00:15:09,062 --> 00:15:11,979
Researchers who study social comparison have found

475
00:15:11,979 --> 00:15:14,546
that people judge their situation largely by

476
00:15:14,546 --> 00:15:15,888
the people around them.

477
00:15:16,068 --> 00:15:18,027
That's useful when the people around you

478
00:15:18,027 --> 00:15:18,762
are doing well.

479
00:15:18,942 --> 00:15:21,879
It's dangerous when they're all quietly struggling.

480
00:15:22,059 --> 00:15:24,095
The comfort of being average can keep

481
00:15:24,095 --> 00:15:25,635
you from noticing real risk.

482
00:15:25,985 --> 00:15:27,006
The fix.

483
00:15:27,186 --> 00:15:29,332
Compare yourself with a standard,

484
00:15:29,332 --> 00:15:30,112
not a crowd.

485
00:15:30,292 --> 00:15:32,544
Can you cover three months of expenses?

486
00:15:32,724 --> 00:15:34,549
Are you saving at least ten to

487
00:15:34,549 --> 00:15:35,522
fifteen percent?

488
00:15:35,702 --> 00:15:37,328
Do you have a plan for retirement?

489
00:15:37,508 --> 00:15:39,615
Those questions don't care what your neighbors

490
00:15:39,615 --> 00:15:40,074
are doing.

491
00:15:40,424 --> 00:15:41,515
Quick pause.

492
00:15:41,695 --> 00:15:43,434
If any of these signs hit close

493
00:15:43,434 --> 00:15:44,948
to home, I want you to know

494
00:15:44,948 --> 00:15:46,351
there's no judgment here.

495
00:15:46,531 --> 00:15:48,356
I make these videos so people can

496
00:15:48,356 --> 00:15:49,572
see the problem early.

497
00:15:49,752 --> 00:15:51,419
If that's useful to you,

498
00:15:51,419 --> 00:15:53,502
subscribe to Ryan Rover Money.

499
00:15:53,682 --> 00:15:55,268
It's free and it helps the channel

500
00:15:55,268 --> 00:15:56,295
more than you'd think.

501
00:15:56,475 --> 00:15:57,961
Now, sign twelve.

502
00:15:58,311 --> 00:15:59,611
Sign number twelve.

503
00:15:59,791 --> 00:16:02,435
You feel anxious checking your bank balance,

504
00:16:02,435 --> 00:16:03,216
so you don't.

505
00:16:03,566 --> 00:16:05,446
Money anxiety is very common.

506
00:16:05,626 --> 00:16:09,609
Surveys from organizations like the American Psychological

507
00:16:09,609 --> 00:16:12,836
Association have consistently found money to be

508
00:16:12,836 --> 00:16:15,033
one of the top sources of stress

509
00:16:15,033 --> 00:16:15,994
for Americans.

510
00:16:16,174 --> 00:16:18,403
When checking your account brings dread,

511
00:16:18,403 --> 00:16:19,796
it's natural to avoid it.

512
00:16:20,147 --> 00:16:21,958
But avoidance makes things worse.

513
00:16:22,138 --> 00:16:24,596
Unchecked accounts lead to overdrafts,

514
00:16:24,596 --> 00:16:27,443
missed fraud, and spending that quietly runs

515
00:16:27,443 --> 00:16:28,802
ahead of your income.

516
00:16:28,982 --> 00:16:31,144
The anxiety is often a signal that,

517
00:16:31,144 --> 00:16:33,801
deep down, you already know something isn't

518
00:16:33,801 --> 00:16:34,172
right.

519
00:16:34,522 --> 00:16:35,404
The fix.

520
00:16:35,584 --> 00:16:37,616
Make checking small and routine.

521
00:16:37,796 --> 00:16:40,176
Five minutes, the same time each week.

522
00:16:40,356 --> 00:16:41,615
Look at the balance,

523
00:16:41,615 --> 00:16:43,252
scan for anything strange,

524
00:16:43,252 --> 00:16:44,071
and close it.

525
00:16:44,251 --> 00:16:47,235
Over time, familiarity replaces fear.

526
00:16:47,415 --> 00:16:48,788
And as your cushion grows,

527
00:16:48,788 --> 00:16:50,689
the anxiety usually shrinks with it.

528
00:16:51,039 --> 00:16:52,513
Sign number thirteen.

529
00:16:52,693 --> 00:16:54,549
You use credit cards to keep up

530
00:16:54,549 --> 00:16:55,387
a normal life.

531
00:16:55,737 --> 00:16:57,107
Groceries on the card.

532
00:16:57,287 --> 00:16:58,482
Gas on the card.

533
00:16:58,662 --> 00:17:00,172
A friend's wedding on the card.

534
00:17:00,352 --> 00:17:02,690
When credit pays for everyday life,

535
00:17:02,690 --> 00:17:04,093
not just emergencies,

536
00:17:04,093 --> 00:17:06,766
it means your income isn't covering your

537
00:17:06,766 --> 00:17:07,434
lifestyle.

538
00:17:07,614 --> 00:17:09,212
The card is filling the gap,

539
00:17:09,212 --> 00:17:11,495
and charging you over twenty percent for

540
00:17:11,495 --> 00:17:11,666
it.

541
00:17:12,016 --> 00:17:14,324
This sign is especially hard to see

542
00:17:14,324 --> 00:17:16,567
because nothing feels extravagant.

543
00:17:16,747 --> 00:17:18,024
You're not buying yachts.

544
00:17:18,204 --> 00:17:19,992
You're buying normal things.

545
00:17:20,172 --> 00:17:22,746
But normal things paid with expensive debt

546
00:17:22,746 --> 00:17:25,197
slowly turn into a heavy monthly payment

547
00:17:25,197 --> 00:17:27,220
that takes away even more of your

548
00:17:27,220 --> 00:17:27,649
income.

549
00:17:27,999 --> 00:17:29,055
The fix.

550
00:17:29,235 --> 00:17:30,367
For one month,

551
00:17:30,367 --> 00:17:33,198
use only debit or cash for everyday

552
00:17:33,198 --> 00:17:33,926
spending.

553
00:17:34,106 --> 00:17:35,186
It will feel tight,

554
00:17:35,186 --> 00:17:36,381
and that's the point.

555
00:17:36,561 --> 00:17:38,798
The tightness shows you the real size

556
00:17:38,798 --> 00:17:39,464
of the gap.

557
00:17:39,644 --> 00:17:41,847
Then close the gap from both sides,

558
00:17:41,847 --> 00:17:44,176
cutting where you can and looking for

559
00:17:44,176 --> 00:17:45,309
ways to earn more.

560
00:17:45,660 --> 00:17:47,088
Sign number fourteen.

561
00:17:47,268 --> 00:17:49,009
Money is always on your mind.

562
00:17:49,359 --> 00:17:53,403
Researchers Sendhil Mullainathan and Eldar Shafir wrote

563
00:17:53,403 --> 00:17:55,094
a book called Scarcity.

564
00:17:55,274 --> 00:17:57,165
They found that when people are short

565
00:17:57,165 --> 00:17:58,954
on money, the worry takes up mental

566
00:17:58,954 --> 00:17:59,465
bandwidth.

567
00:17:59,645 --> 00:18:00,981
It's harder to focus,

568
00:18:00,981 --> 00:18:01,935
harder to plan,

569
00:18:01,935 --> 00:18:04,416
and easier to make short term decisions

570
00:18:04,416 --> 00:18:05,752
that cost more later.

571
00:18:06,102 --> 00:18:08,862
That means being financially stretched doesn't just

572
00:18:08,862 --> 00:18:09,457
cost money.

573
00:18:09,637 --> 00:18:10,960
It costs attention.

574
00:18:11,140 --> 00:18:13,580
People who are always worried about the

575
00:18:13,580 --> 00:18:15,708
next bill have less energy for the

576
00:18:15,708 --> 00:18:16,709
long term moves,

577
00:18:16,709 --> 00:18:19,211
like learning a new skill or negotiating

578
00:18:19,211 --> 00:18:21,776
a raise, that could actually change their

579
00:18:21,776 --> 00:18:22,402
situation.

580
00:18:22,752 --> 00:18:23,773
The fix.

581
00:18:23,953 --> 00:18:25,370
Create a small buffer,

582
00:18:25,370 --> 00:18:27,109
even a few hundred dollars,

583
00:18:27,109 --> 00:18:28,075
and protect it.

584
00:18:28,255 --> 00:18:30,472
Many people find that even a small

585
00:18:30,472 --> 00:18:33,342
cushion reduces the constant worry and frees

586
00:18:33,342 --> 00:18:34,385
up mental space.

587
00:18:34,565 --> 00:18:36,467
Then use that space to make one

588
00:18:36,467 --> 00:18:38,001
long term move at a time.

589
00:18:38,352 --> 00:18:40,763
Why are these psychological signs so hard

590
00:18:40,763 --> 00:18:41,939
to see in ourselves?

591
00:18:42,119 --> 00:18:44,325
Part of it is optimism bias.

592
00:18:44,505 --> 00:18:46,927
Research shows that most people tend to

593
00:18:46,927 --> 00:18:49,224
believe bad things are more likely to

594
00:18:49,224 --> 00:18:51,459
happen to others than to themselves.

595
00:18:51,639 --> 00:18:53,763
Job losses, illnesses,

596
00:18:53,763 --> 00:18:54,728
accidents.

597
00:18:54,908 --> 00:18:55,883
We know they happen.

598
00:18:56,063 --> 00:18:57,926
We just don't expect them to happen

599
00:18:57,926 --> 00:18:58,246
to us.

600
00:18:58,426 --> 00:19:01,120
That's why emergency funds feel optional until

601
00:19:01,120 --> 00:19:02,292
the day they're not.

602
00:19:02,642 --> 00:19:05,326
Another part is that wealth is invisible

603
00:19:05,326 --> 00:19:06,937
and spending is visible.

604
00:19:07,117 --> 00:19:08,631
You can see a new car in

605
00:19:08,631 --> 00:19:09,451
the driveway.

606
00:19:09,631 --> 00:19:11,820
You can't see an emergency fund or

607
00:19:11,820 --> 00:19:13,172
a retirement account.

608
00:19:13,352 --> 00:19:16,028
So the signals around us constantly reward

609
00:19:16,028 --> 00:19:18,577
spending and almost never reward saving.

610
00:19:18,757 --> 00:19:21,256
Nobody compliments your index fund at a

611
00:19:21,256 --> 00:19:22,089
dinner party.

612
00:19:22,439 --> 00:19:24,739
The answer is to make your progress

613
00:19:24,739 --> 00:19:26,711
visible, at least to yourself.

614
00:19:26,891 --> 00:19:29,028
Some people use a chart on the

615
00:19:29,028 --> 00:19:29,527
fridge.

616
00:19:29,707 --> 00:19:32,874
Some rename savings accounts after their goals,

617
00:19:32,874 --> 00:19:34,896
like freedom fund or new roof.

618
00:19:35,076 --> 00:19:37,874
Some celebrate every thousand dollars saved.

619
00:19:38,054 --> 00:19:39,778
When progress is visible,

620
00:19:39,778 --> 00:19:42,328
saving starts to feel as rewarding as

621
00:19:42,328 --> 00:19:43,569
spending once did.

622
00:19:43,919 --> 00:19:44,882
That's angle two.

623
00:19:45,062 --> 00:19:47,595
Payday highs, stuff as a scoreboard,

624
00:19:47,595 --> 00:19:49,142
spending future money,

625
00:19:49,142 --> 00:19:50,901
comparing with the crowd,

626
00:19:50,901 --> 00:19:54,137
avoidance, credit for normal life and constant

627
00:19:54,137 --> 00:19:54,559
worry.

628
00:19:54,739 --> 00:19:55,899
Now angle three,

629
00:19:55,899 --> 00:19:56,841
the playbook.

630
00:19:57,021 --> 00:19:58,801
These are the weak spots in your

631
00:19:58,801 --> 00:19:59,412
safety net.

632
00:19:59,592 --> 00:20:01,403
And this is where we fix things.

633
00:20:01,754 --> 00:20:04,006
Think of your finances like a house.

634
00:20:04,186 --> 00:20:05,544
Income is the roof.

635
00:20:05,724 --> 00:20:07,315
Savings are the walls.

636
00:20:07,495 --> 00:20:09,353
Insurance is the foundation.

637
00:20:09,533 --> 00:20:11,417
Most people focus on the roof and

638
00:20:11,417 --> 00:20:12,331
forget the rest.

639
00:20:12,511 --> 00:20:14,776
These last seven signs are cracks in

640
00:20:14,776 --> 00:20:16,915
the foundation, and each one has a

641
00:20:16,915 --> 00:20:18,362
straightforward repair.

642
00:20:18,712 --> 00:20:20,198
Sign number fifteen.

643
00:20:20,378 --> 00:20:22,377
Losing your job would be a crisis

644
00:20:22,377 --> 00:20:23,164
within weeks.

645
00:20:23,514 --> 00:20:25,278
Ask yourself a simple question.

646
00:20:25,458 --> 00:20:27,120
If your income stopped tomorrow,

647
00:20:27,120 --> 00:20:28,988
how many months could you cover your

648
00:20:28,988 --> 00:20:29,975
essential expenses?

649
00:20:30,155 --> 00:20:32,402
Rent, food, utilities,

650
00:20:32,402 --> 00:20:35,263
insurance, minimum payments.

651
00:20:35,443 --> 00:20:36,743
For many households,

652
00:20:36,743 --> 00:20:38,954
the honest answer is less than one

653
00:20:38,954 --> 00:20:39,344
month.

654
00:20:39,694 --> 00:20:42,260
Job losses happen even to good workers.

655
00:20:42,440 --> 00:20:45,335
Companies restructure, industries change,

656
00:20:45,335 --> 00:20:46,747
and recessions come.

657
00:20:46,927 --> 00:20:50,303
The Bureau of Labor Statistics reports millions

658
00:20:50,303 --> 00:20:52,961
of layoffs and discharges every year,

659
00:20:52,961 --> 00:20:54,613
even in good economies.

660
00:20:54,793 --> 00:20:57,369
Your job security depends partly on things

661
00:20:57,369 --> 00:20:58,473
you can't control.

662
00:20:58,653 --> 00:21:00,151
Your cushion depends on you.

663
00:21:00,501 --> 00:21:01,325
The fix.

664
00:21:01,505 --> 00:21:04,187
Calculate your essential monthly expenses.

665
00:21:04,367 --> 00:21:05,632
Multiply by three.

666
00:21:05,812 --> 00:21:07,148
That's your medium term goal.

667
00:21:07,328 --> 00:21:09,160
Six months is even better if your

668
00:21:09,160 --> 00:21:11,269
income is irregular or you're the only

669
00:21:11,269 --> 00:21:11,658
earner.

670
00:21:11,838 --> 00:21:13,455
Build it step by step,

671
00:21:13,455 --> 00:21:15,659
and keep it in an FDIC insured

672
00:21:15,659 --> 00:21:17,643
high yield savings account.

673
00:21:17,993 --> 00:21:19,525
Sign number sixteen.

674
00:21:19,705 --> 00:21:22,097
All your income comes from one place.

675
00:21:22,447 --> 00:21:24,664
Most people depend on a single paycheck.

676
00:21:24,844 --> 00:21:26,969
That's normal, and it's not wrong.

677
00:21:27,149 --> 00:21:29,017
But it means one decision by one

678
00:21:29,017 --> 00:21:31,119
employer can change your whole life.

679
00:21:31,299 --> 00:21:33,756
People who build wealth often add a

680
00:21:33,756 --> 00:21:35,441
second stream over time,

681
00:21:35,441 --> 00:21:36,915
like a side business,

682
00:21:36,915 --> 00:21:40,495
freelance work, rental income or investment income.

683
00:21:40,845 --> 00:21:43,090
A second income stream doesn't have to

684
00:21:43,090 --> 00:21:43,503
be big.

685
00:21:43,683 --> 00:21:45,769
Even a few hundred dollars a month

686
00:21:45,769 --> 00:21:48,100
adds resilience, and if you invest it,

687
00:21:48,100 --> 00:21:48,652
it grows.

688
00:21:48,832 --> 00:21:51,491
It also builds skills and contacts that

689
00:21:51,491 --> 00:21:53,877
make you more valuable in your main

690
00:21:53,877 --> 00:21:54,150
job.

691
00:21:54,500 --> 00:21:55,312
The fix.

692
00:21:55,492 --> 00:21:57,593
List three skills people might pay you

693
00:21:57,593 --> 00:21:57,814
for.

694
00:21:57,994 --> 00:21:59,668
Pick one and test it with a

695
00:21:59,668 --> 00:22:01,652
single small project this month.

696
00:22:01,832 --> 00:22:04,662
Keep your expectations modest and your costs

697
00:22:04,662 --> 00:22:04,920
low.

698
00:22:05,100 --> 00:22:05,963
If it works,

699
00:22:05,963 --> 00:22:08,120
put most of what you earn into

700
00:22:08,120 --> 00:22:09,774
savings or investments,

701
00:22:09,774 --> 00:22:11,787
not into a bigger lifestyle.

702
00:22:12,137 --> 00:22:13,704
Sign number seventeen.

703
00:22:13,884 --> 00:22:15,823
You're missing basic insurance.

704
00:22:16,173 --> 00:22:17,542
No health insurance,

705
00:22:17,542 --> 00:22:18,979
no renters insurance,

706
00:22:18,979 --> 00:22:20,759
the minimum car insurance,

707
00:22:20,759 --> 00:22:22,333
no disability coverage,

708
00:22:22,333 --> 00:22:25,071
and no life insurance even though people

709
00:22:25,071 --> 00:22:26,030
depend on you.

710
00:22:26,210 --> 00:22:29,432
Insurance feels like money thrown away until

711
00:22:29,432 --> 00:22:30,970
something goes wrong.

712
00:22:31,150 --> 00:22:33,097
Medical bills are one of the most

713
00:22:33,097 --> 00:22:35,457
common reasons Americans fall into debt.

714
00:22:35,807 --> 00:22:38,879
Disability insurance is the one people forget

715
00:22:38,879 --> 00:22:39,220
most.

716
00:22:39,400 --> 00:22:41,589
Your ability to earn an income over

717
00:22:41,589 --> 00:22:44,466
your career is probably your biggest financial

718
00:22:44,466 --> 00:22:46,655
asset, and an illness or injury can

719
00:22:46,655 --> 00:22:47,156
stop it.

720
00:22:47,336 --> 00:22:50,392
Many employers offer disability coverage,

721
00:22:50,392 --> 00:22:52,107
sometimes very cheaply.

722
00:22:52,457 --> 00:22:53,304
The fix.

723
00:22:53,484 --> 00:22:55,717
List every type of insurance you have

724
00:22:55,717 --> 00:22:56,863
and what it covers.

725
00:22:57,043 --> 00:22:58,738
Check for the big gaps first.

726
00:22:58,918 --> 00:23:01,125
Health, disability if you work,

727
00:23:01,125 --> 00:23:03,831
life if anyone depends on your income,

728
00:23:03,831 --> 00:23:04,827
and liability.

729
00:23:05,007 --> 00:23:08,365
Renters insurance is often quite inexpensive and

730
00:23:08,365 --> 00:23:10,534
covers more than people expect.

731
00:23:10,884 --> 00:23:12,405
Sign number eighteen.

732
00:23:12,585 --> 00:23:14,813
Almost all your wealth is in your

733
00:23:14,813 --> 00:23:15,150
home.

734
00:23:15,501 --> 00:23:17,614
Home equity counts toward net worth.

735
00:23:17,794 --> 00:23:19,082
But it's hard to use.

736
00:23:19,262 --> 00:23:21,064
You can't spend a bedroom at the

737
00:23:21,064 --> 00:23:21,852
grocery store.

738
00:23:22,032 --> 00:23:24,066
To turn home equity into cash,

739
00:23:24,066 --> 00:23:26,371
you usually need to sell or borrow

740
00:23:26,371 --> 00:23:27,117
against it.

741
00:23:27,297 --> 00:23:29,996
Many people reach their fifties or sixties

742
00:23:29,996 --> 00:23:32,374
with a valuable house and very little

743
00:23:32,374 --> 00:23:32,695
else.

744
00:23:33,045 --> 00:23:34,861
A home is a place to live

745
00:23:34,861 --> 00:23:35,297
first.

746
00:23:35,477 --> 00:23:38,311
It's also concentrated in one property,

747
00:23:38,311 --> 00:23:41,073
one neighborhood and one local market.

748
00:23:41,253 --> 00:23:44,086
Investments like broad index funds spread risk

749
00:23:44,086 --> 00:23:45,934
across thousands of companies,

750
00:23:45,934 --> 00:23:48,459
and they can provide income in retirement

751
00:23:48,459 --> 00:23:50,553
without needing to sell your home.

752
00:23:50,903 --> 00:23:51,715
The fix.

753
00:23:51,895 --> 00:23:54,617
Make sure you're investing outside your home,

754
00:23:54,617 --> 00:23:57,700
ideally through tax advantaged retirement accounts.

755
00:23:57,880 --> 00:23:59,932
Even if your house is your biggest

756
00:23:59,932 --> 00:24:01,924
asset, aim to build a second pile

757
00:24:01,924 --> 00:24:04,278
of wealth that you can actually access.

758
00:24:04,628 --> 00:24:06,125
Sign number nineteen.

759
00:24:06,305 --> 00:24:08,855
You pay for convenience you can't really

760
00:24:08,855 --> 00:24:09,301
afford.

761
00:24:09,651 --> 00:24:11,888
Delivery fees, ride shares,

762
00:24:11,888 --> 00:24:15,369
premium subscriptions, pre cut vegetables,

763
00:24:15,369 --> 00:24:16,861
expensive coffees.

764
00:24:17,041 --> 00:24:19,511
Convenience is wonderful when you have room

765
00:24:19,511 --> 00:24:20,373
in your budget.

766
00:24:20,553 --> 00:24:21,441
When you don't,

767
00:24:21,441 --> 00:24:23,572
it becomes a quiet premium on almost

768
00:24:23,572 --> 00:24:24,697
everything you buy.

769
00:24:25,048 --> 00:24:27,555
The tricky part is that convenience often

770
00:24:27,555 --> 00:24:29,939
feels like a necessity when you're busy

771
00:24:29,939 --> 00:24:30,551
and tired.

772
00:24:30,731 --> 00:24:33,193
And many people who are stretched financially

773
00:24:33,193 --> 00:24:34,725
are also stretched for time.

774
00:24:34,905 --> 00:24:37,383
That makes convenience spending one of the

775
00:24:37,383 --> 00:24:38,858
hardest habits to change,

776
00:24:38,858 --> 00:24:40,628
and one of the most expensive.

777
00:24:40,979 --> 00:24:41,931
The fix.

778
00:24:42,111 --> 00:24:45,034
Pick the single most expensive convenience in

779
00:24:45,034 --> 00:24:46,983
your budget and cut it in half

780
00:24:46,983 --> 00:24:47,893
for one month.

781
00:24:48,073 --> 00:24:51,059
For example, delivery twice a week instead

782
00:24:51,059 --> 00:24:52,055
of four times.

783
00:24:52,235 --> 00:24:54,635
Put the difference into savings automatically,

784
00:24:54,635 --> 00:24:56,200
so you can see what the change

785
00:24:56,200 --> 00:24:56,670
is worth.

786
00:24:57,020 --> 00:24:58,320
Sign number twenty.

787
00:24:58,500 --> 00:25:00,599
Your total debt is higher than it

788
00:25:00,599 --> 00:25:01,554
was a year ago.

789
00:25:01,904 --> 00:25:03,657
Debt isn't automatically bad.

790
00:25:03,837 --> 00:25:06,446
A reasonable mortgage or student loan can

791
00:25:06,446 --> 00:25:07,146
make sense.

792
00:25:07,326 --> 00:25:08,854
But if your total debt,

793
00:25:08,854 --> 00:25:10,715
not counting a new mortgage,

794
00:25:10,715 --> 00:25:12,643
keeps rising year after year,

795
00:25:12,643 --> 00:25:15,766
it usually means spending is outrunning income.

796
00:25:15,946 --> 00:25:18,848
And rising debt means rising interest payments,

797
00:25:18,848 --> 00:25:21,194
which take even more income next year.

798
00:25:21,544 --> 00:25:24,185
The New York Fed's household debt reports

799
00:25:24,185 --> 00:25:27,019
show total American household debt at record

800
00:25:27,019 --> 00:25:28,500
levels in recent years,

801
00:25:28,500 --> 00:25:30,948
including credit cards and auto loans.

802
00:25:31,128 --> 00:25:32,799
That doesn't mean your debt has to

803
00:25:32,799 --> 00:25:34,077
follow the national trend.

804
00:25:34,257 --> 00:25:35,914
The direction of your own debt is

805
00:25:35,914 --> 00:25:37,722
one of the clearest signals of where

806
00:25:37,722 --> 00:25:38,425
you're headed.

807
00:25:38,775 --> 00:25:39,634
The fix.

808
00:25:39,814 --> 00:25:41,189
Write down every debt,

809
00:25:41,189 --> 00:25:42,751
its balance and its rate.

810
00:25:42,931 --> 00:25:44,821
Next year, you want that total to

811
00:25:44,821 --> 00:25:45,451
be smaller.

812
00:25:45,631 --> 00:25:47,118
Choose a payoff method,

813
00:25:47,118 --> 00:25:49,380
like paying the highest rate first,

814
00:25:49,380 --> 00:25:51,320
and automate an extra payment,

815
00:25:51,320 --> 00:25:52,225
however small.

816
00:25:52,575 --> 00:25:54,305
And sign number twenty one.

817
00:25:54,485 --> 00:25:56,226
You don't have a written money plan.

818
00:25:56,576 --> 00:25:58,094
No budget, no goals,

819
00:25:58,094 --> 00:26:00,372
no idea when you could retire.

820
00:26:00,552 --> 00:26:02,154
Just hoping things work out.

821
00:26:02,334 --> 00:26:04,532
Hope is wonderful for motivation,

822
00:26:04,532 --> 00:26:07,129
but it's a terrible financial strategy.

823
00:26:07,309 --> 00:26:08,188
Without a plan,

824
00:26:08,188 --> 00:26:10,355
every decision happens in the moment,

825
00:26:10,355 --> 00:26:12,580
and the moment usually wants to spend.

826
00:26:12,930 --> 00:26:15,066
A plan doesn't have to be complex.

827
00:26:15,246 --> 00:26:16,999
It can fit on one page.

828
00:26:17,179 --> 00:26:18,014
What comes in,

829
00:26:18,014 --> 00:26:18,849
what goes out,

830
00:26:18,849 --> 00:26:19,683
what you save,

831
00:26:19,683 --> 00:26:21,055
what you're paying off,

832
00:26:21,055 --> 00:26:22,903
and what you're working toward.

833
00:26:23,083 --> 00:26:25,336
People who write down their goals tend

834
00:26:25,336 --> 00:26:26,996
to take them more seriously,

835
00:26:26,996 --> 00:26:29,070
because a written goal is a promise

836
00:26:29,070 --> 00:26:29,782
to yourself.

837
00:26:30,132 --> 00:26:31,095
The fix.

838
00:26:31,275 --> 00:26:33,087
Write a one page plan this week.

839
00:26:33,267 --> 00:26:34,793
Income, fixed costs,

840
00:26:34,793 --> 00:26:38,076
savings, debts and three goals with numbers

841
00:26:38,076 --> 00:26:38,839
and dates.

842
00:26:39,019 --> 00:26:40,343
Review it once a month.

843
00:26:40,523 --> 00:26:41,603
Update it once a year.

844
00:26:41,783 --> 00:26:43,923
That one page can change the direction

845
00:26:43,923 --> 00:26:45,556
of your whole financial life.

846
00:26:45,906 --> 00:26:47,597
Let me tell you a story that

847
00:26:47,597 --> 00:26:49,711
shows how these signs hide in plain

848
00:26:49,711 --> 00:26:50,074
sight.

849
00:26:50,424 --> 00:26:52,711
Imagine a couple in their late thirties.

850
00:26:52,891 --> 00:26:54,749
Let's call them Chris and Dana.

851
00:26:54,929 --> 00:26:55,806
Two good jobs,

852
00:26:55,806 --> 00:26:58,438
combined income of about one hundred forty

853
00:26:58,438 --> 00:26:59,503
thousand dollars.

854
00:26:59,683 --> 00:27:01,589
A nice home in the suburbs,

855
00:27:01,589 --> 00:27:02,649
two newer cars,

856
00:27:02,649 --> 00:27:04,838
a family vacation every summer.

857
00:27:05,018 --> 00:27:06,957
Their friends think they're doing great.

858
00:27:07,137 --> 00:27:08,762
Honestly, so do they.

859
00:27:09,112 --> 00:27:11,782
Then Chris's company announces layoffs.

860
00:27:11,962 --> 00:27:14,231
